WEBVTT

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.141
What does the boundary measure?

00:00:02.141 --> 00:00:09.374
In Lesson six, we bounded a Legendre coefficient using the largest magnitude of the function on the outer ellipse.

00:00:09.374 --> 00:00:13.684
Now replace that measurement with the root mean square over a full turn.

00:00:13.684 --> 00:00:17.589
Follow the same function, omega squared, through both measurements.

00:00:17.589 --> 00:00:22.383
They lead to different constants and the same geometric decay with degree.

00:00:22.383 --> 00:00:24.435
One angle locates two points.

00:00:24.435 --> 00:00:27.380
Choose the outer circle of radius one and a half.

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:29.663
The cyan point is z on that circle.

00:00:29.663 --> 00:00:35.466
The Joukowski map sends it to the gold point omega on the boundary of the violet ellipse.

00:00:35.466 --> 00:00:38.347
Move the two points by increasing the same angle.

00:00:38.347 --> 00:00:42.252
Equal steps on the circle cover different lengths along the ellipse.

00:00:42.252 --> 00:00:46.555
Each angular step still gets equal weight in the Hardy integral.

00:00:46.555 --> 00:00:48.586
Each height contributes to the mean.

00:00:48.586 --> 00:00:53.749
Raise a cyan stem over the ellipse point by the squared magnitude of omega squared.

00:00:53.749 --> 00:00:58.401
As the point travels, the stem traces a ridge, tallest at the real tips.

00:00:58.401 --> 00:01:03.607
The matching graph unwraps one circuit and shades the accumulated angular energy.

00:01:03.607 --> 00:01:08.877
Divide the full area by two pi to get the mean, about zero point five seven nine.

00:01:08.877 --> 00:01:15.783
Its square root, about zero point seven six one, is the Hardy norm of this function on the outer ellipse.

00:01:16.593 --> 00:01:18.859
Recover the same real coefficient.

00:01:18.859 --> 00:01:25.814
On the red interval, x squared is one third of the constant mode plus two thirds of the second Legendre polynomial.

00:01:25.814 --> 00:01:28.759
Our target coefficient is therefore two thirds.

00:01:28.759 --> 00:01:36.823
Lesson four, Bandtlow’s Lemma one point seven, recovers that same number by circling the violet ellipse once counterclockwise.

00:01:36.823 --> 00:01:43.175
The second kind kernel carries information about the degree two polynomial into the contour integral.

00:01:43.175 --> 00:01:45.547
The moving tangent supplies direction.

00:01:45.547 --> 00:01:48.598
Follow the arrow attached to the gold boundary point.

00:01:48.598 --> 00:01:53.442
It is the derivative of omega with respect to angle, drawn with one fixed scale.

00:01:53.442 --> 00:01:57.666
Multiply the function and kernel values by that complex tangent.

00:01:57.666 --> 00:02:03.747
The curve on the right adds these directed contributions in order, including the contour prefactor.

00:02:03.747 --> 00:02:07.204
The moving tip can leave the real axis during the circuit.

00:02:07.204 --> 00:02:11.422
It returns to the real value two thirds when the circuit closes.

00:02:11.422 --> 00:02:13.879
Cauchy–Schwarz separates two energies.

00:02:13.879 --> 00:02:17.549
Read the first squared magnitude under the angular integral.

00:02:17.549 --> 00:02:19.193
It measures the function alone.

00:02:19.193 --> 00:02:24.633
The other integral measures the squared magnitude of the kernel multiplied by the tangent.

00:02:24.633 --> 00:02:29.946
Their square roots bound the coefficient after multiplication by two n plus one.

00:02:29.946 --> 00:02:33.296
For this example the actual coefficient is two thirds.

00:02:33.296 --> 00:02:36.689
The energy estimate is about zero point nine three one.

00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:42.741
Replacing the kernel energy by a formula valid at every degree will give a looser bound.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.484
Build the kernel from three waves.

00:02:45.484 --> 00:02:50.221
Lesson five, Bandtlow’s Lemma one point eight, gives the one-sided ladder.

00:02:50.221 --> 00:02:55.917
For degree two, the first three frequencies are minus three, minus five, and minus seven.

00:02:55.917 --> 00:03:00.995
Each coloured arrow turns clockwise and adds one term to the kernel approximation.

00:03:00.995 --> 00:03:07.908
Its length is the real coefficient sigma multiplied by the outer radius to the negative frequency index.

00:03:07.908 --> 00:03:10.633
The gold tip follows the finite sum.

00:03:10.633 --> 00:03:12.472
Select a pair of frequencies.

00:03:12.472 --> 00:03:17.806
Multiply the three-term sum by its complex conjugate and the squared boundary speed.

00:03:17.806 --> 00:03:25.124
Row three and column five select a product with amplitude c three times c five and phase twice the angle.

00:03:25.124 --> 00:03:30.479
The speed squared has a constant wave and two waves with frequencies plus and minus two.

00:03:30.479 --> 00:03:32.954
The minus two wave cancels this pair’s phase.

00:03:32.954 --> 00:03:37.450
Its constant contribution survives averaging over the full circuit.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:39.864
Three diagonals survive a full turn.

00:03:39.864 --> 00:03:44.857
The main diagonal contributes a positive multiple of each squared amplitude.

00:03:44.857 --> 00:03:49.487
Each adjacent cell contributes minus one quarter of a neighbouring product.

00:03:49.487 --> 00:03:51.963
The reflected cell makes a second copy.

00:03:51.963 --> 00:03:59.942
For three terms, subtract about zero point zero one zero zero five from zero point zero six nine nine four.

00:03:59.942 --> 00:04:05.681
The resulting zero point zero five nine eight nine is the energy of the three-term packet.

00:04:05.681 --> 00:04:09.643
Adding further kernel terms changes this finite energy.

00:04:09.643 --> 00:04:12.037
Turn the exact identity into a bound.

00:04:12.037 --> 00:04:19.611
For the full ladder, every surviving coefficient is nonnegative, so discarding the subtracted correction gives an upper bound.

00:04:19.611 --> 00:04:21.233
Each sigma is at most two.

00:04:21.233 --> 00:04:28.978
Squaring the radial factors makes the remaining geometric sum decrease by a factor of R to the minus four at every step.

00:04:28.978 --> 00:04:31.986
Its sum gives the explicit constant C two of R.

00:04:31.986 --> 00:04:36.439
Bandtlow’s Lemma one point ten follows by taking the square root.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:39.355
Extend the estimate by density.

00:04:39.355 --> 00:04:45.883
We first wrote the contour formula for functions continuous up to the outer boundary and holomorphic inside.

00:04:45.883 --> 00:04:50.428
Approximate a Hardy input in the outer Hardy norm by such functions.

00:04:50.428 --> 00:04:54.738
The coefficient bound makes their coefficient values a Cauchy sequence.

00:04:54.738 --> 00:04:59.240
Its limit defines the coefficient continuously for every Hardy input.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:05.826
For its holomorphic representative, this is the same coefficient that we recover from the red interval.

00:05:05.826 --> 00:05:07.793
Two ellipses supply two powers.

00:05:07.793 --> 00:05:12.466
Keep the violet outer analytic ellipse fixed at R equal to one and a half.

00:05:12.466 --> 00:05:16.349
Choose the gold target ellipse with r equal to one point two.

00:05:16.349 --> 00:05:21.150
The coefficient estimate supplies R to the minus n from the outer Hardy norm.

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:29.662
Lesson three, Bandtlow’s Lemma one point six, bounds the magnitude of the basis polynomial by r to the n throughout the inner ellipse.

00:05:29.662 --> 00:05:31.327
Multiply the two powers.

00:05:31.327 --> 00:05:35.502
Each term now carries the ratio zero point eight to the n.

00:05:35.502 --> 00:05:37.512
Sum every discarded degree.

00:05:37.512 --> 00:05:42.099
Keep the real-line Legendre modes of degrees zero through N minus one.

00:05:42.099 --> 00:05:44.212
The first omitted bar is degree N.

00:05:44.212 --> 00:05:49.887
Sum the upper envelopes from that bar onward, including the infinitely many bars beyond the chart.

00:05:49.887 --> 00:05:55.285
Moving the cutoff to the right drives the bound to zero throughout the fixed closed inner ellipse.

00:05:55.285 --> 00:06:00.619
With omega squared as input, the actual error vanishes as soon as three modes are kept.

00:06:00.619 --> 00:06:05.755
The positive bound still applies to all inputs of the same outer Hardy norm.

00:06:05.755 --> 00:06:07.807
Follow the two measurements together.

00:06:07.807 --> 00:06:14.250
On the outer ellipse, the rotating point measures the function energy and the tangent-weighted kernel energy.

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:17.344
The three diagonals evaluate that kernel energy.

00:06:17.344 --> 00:06:21.612
The resulting coefficient bound shrinks with degree on the outer circle.

00:06:21.612 --> 00:06:24.684
On the smaller target ellipse the basis can grow.

00:06:24.684 --> 00:06:28.034
Their ratio controls the uniform reconstruction error.

00:06:28.034 --> 00:06:36.540
If the two radii coincide, the ratio becomes one and this geometric tail argument no longer supplies convergence.

